[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125060,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Acral naevus is a benign melanocytic neoplasm of the volar skin of hands or feet or the nail apparatus.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:57:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125061,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8744/0 Acral naevus</p>\r\n<p>8744/0 Nail matrix naevus</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:57:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125062,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F20.Y &amp; XH9DB2 Other specific types of melanocytic naevus &amp; Acral naevus</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:57:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125063,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> naevi of special sites; melanocytic acral naevus with intraepidermal ascent of cells.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> atypical lentiginous naevus; acral lentiginous naevus.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:57:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125064,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Nail matrix naevus</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:57:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125065,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Acral naevi occur on the palms, soles, and volar surface of the fingers and toes. They involve non&ndash;weight-bearing areas of the sole in adults and the forefoot in children {28676327; 30055202}. Nail matrix naevus (NMN) occurs on the nail matrix, and the thumbnail is the most common site {8632071; 27696528; 29107341}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:57:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125066,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Acral naevi are brownish to black small macules or flat papules. Most display one of three major dermoscopic patterns: the parallel furrow pattern, the lattice-like pattern, or the fibrillar pattern {9606325; 16021115; 15492186; 21175752}. NMN manifests as longitudinal melanonychia {8632071}. Dermoscopy of NMN typically shows thick parallel lines without colour variation {27696528}, but NMNs may be darker, larger, or multicoloured, and in children they may display Hutchinson&rsquo;s sign {27177439; 31850539}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:57:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125067,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Acral naevi are common, occurring in more than a third of individuals, and their incidence peaks in the third and fourth decades of life {26803347; 19389519}. They are more common in females {26803347} and in individuals with higher constitutional pigmentation and many melanocytic naevi {20956637; 26803347}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:57:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125068,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:57:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125069,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Acral naevi harbour <em>BRAF</em> or <em>NRAS</em> mutations, similarly to common acquired naevi {29191620; 33978681}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:57:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125070,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:57:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125071,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>Acral naevi are relatively small and circumscribed, and they can be junctional, compound, or intradermal {32640104}. Acral skin is a &ldquo;special site&rdquo; where naevi may show features considered atypical in other locations; pagetoid scatter, bridging between rete, and fibroplasia are not uncommon in the centre of the lesion {28676327}. Naevus cells are arranged as single units and variably sized nests, which can be confluent {7929919; 9688763; 27870726}. There may be random cytological atypia {16446715}. Intradermal melanocytes display maturation. Nuclear atypia, confluence of melanocytes, focal pagetoid spread, and periungual involvement are common in NMN in children {29107341; 32314423}.</p>\r\n<p>Melanocytic acral naevus with intraepidermal ascent of cells is a presentation of acral naevus showing pagetoid scatter of nests or single cells, which is more commonly seen in children {31373032; 28676327}.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunohistochemistry</h5>\r\n<p>Acral naevi express melanocytic markers, which can help reveal subtle proliferations in NMN.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>A confluent lentiginous intraepidermal proliferation of atypical melanocytes and a patchy inflammatory infiltrate is more common in acral melanoma than in acral naevus. Strong cyclin&nbsp;D1 expression supports acral melanoma {34225728}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:57:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
